  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Before you even think about lifting those heavy drywall sheets, there are a few critical things to consider that'll save you headaches down the road. First off, you've got to prep the room. This means making sure the framing is solid and all those hidden electrical and plumbing lines are where they need to be and correctly installed. You definitely don't want to be cutting into a pipe or wire after the drywall is up! Next, think about your layout. Residential jobs often hang sheets horizontally to minimize seams, but commercial projects might require vertical hanging for structural reasons or to align with framing. You also need to measure and mark your studs accurately; this ensures your fasteners hit something solid. Don't forget to keep your sheets off the floor—a half-inch gap is usually recommended. And, importantly, always check local building codes for any specific requirements, especially concerning electrical and insulation. Getting these details right upfront is key to a smooth process. If you're feeling overwhelmed, especially with large or complex layouts, bringing in a pro can really make a difference."
    }
  ],
